// Two things a `for` over many entities got wrong when it was built for
// all the cores (Inputs/parallel_calls.ent, 100,000 entities):
//
// - A body that calls a function is run in parallel from fewer entities
//   on than one that only computes. What it added into a unique was then
//   added where it stood, by every thread at once, and some of it was
//   lost: a third of the entities came to fewer than 33,334.
// - A text handed to a proc took a place on the stack for every entity
//   and gave none back until the system was done: after 65,000 lines the
//   stack was full.
// RUN: %ent run %S/Inputs/parallel_calls.ent 2>/dev/null > %t.one
// RUN: %ent run --parallel %S/Inputs/parallel_calls.ent 2>/dev/null > %t.all
// RUN: head -1 %t.one | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=SUM
// RUN: head -1 %t.all | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=SUM
// RUN: tail -1 %t.one | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=LAST
// RUN: tail -1 %t.all | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=LAST
// RUN: cmp %t.one %t.all

// SUM: thirds 33334
// LAST: person 99999
